# Team Kanban lifecycle
Use this as editable starting guidance, not an assertion about an existing board. Preserve established project choices. Installing this document enables no automation.

Issues own shared discussion, tracking and acceptance and are the default board cards; linked PRs are discrete review/integration points. Several PRs may serve one issue. Use issue-only views with Linked pull requests information; separate PR cards need an explicit project exception. Check repository auto-close and native linked/merged-PR rules so partial contributions leave unfinished issues open and preserve Blocked/terminal intent.

| Stage | Starting meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Backlog | Captured work awaiting prioritization. |
| Upcoming | Selected for a future period. |
| On Deck | Ready to start. |
| Active | Work in progress. |
| Blocked | Progress is waiting on an identified condition. |
| Done | The shared outcome meets its acceptance criteria. |

## Selected transitions
- New issues enter Backlog; explicitly reopened issues return to Backlog unless an enabled blocker applies.
- Completed closes move existing cards to Done. A deliberate terminal stage is preserved. Cancellation and other close reasons need their own project decision; the starter automation leaves them alone.
- Select label blocking, dependency blocking or both. If both apply, clearing one does not clear the other. Label clearing can return a Blocked card to On Deck only when no other enabled blocker remains. Dependency removal alone does not auto-unblock.
- Board-driven reopening is optional. If selected, use a ten-minute fresh-close guard as the starting value and a fifteen-minute reconciliation schedule. Preserve an existing guard and advertise scheduler lag.

## Setup record
Record the selected board/repository scope, enabled transitions, field/options, authoritative actors and actual schedule. Keep credentials in the forge's secret store and nonsecret runtime settings in repository/organization variables. Verify the actual native close mapping and competing native workflows before enabling writes. A terminal board stage does not close an agent's spike.
